1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,327
Sometimes a server must not start everything.

2
00:00:03,347 --> 00:00:07,517
Maybe a service is broken, or you need a quiet system to fix it.

3
00:00:07,537 --> 00:00:11,708
Linux lets you choose, at boot, how much of the system to start.

4
00:00:12,178 --> 00:00:15,699
In this video: boot systems into different targets manually.

5
00:00:20,219 --> 00:00:22,802
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:23,272 --> 00:00:25,940
Boot systems into different targets manually.

7
00:00:26,410 --> 00:00:29,618
We'll learn what a target is, see which targets are active,

8
00:00:29,638 --> 00:00:33,558
pass a target to the kernel at boot, and watch the system come up in it.

9
00:00:43,078 --> 00:00:49,065
A target is a goal state for the system: a group of services that should be running.

10
00:00:49,085 --> 00:00:52,283
multi-user.target is a full text-mode system.

11
00:00:52,303 --> 00:00:55,645
graphical.target adds a graphical login on top.

12
00:01:02,115 --> 00:01:06,678
rescue.target is a small system with a root shell, for repairs.

13
00:01:06,698 --> 00:01:12,426
emergency.target is even smaller: only the root file system, mounted read-only.

14
00:01:12,446 --> 00:01:14,681
Both ask for the root password.

15
00:01:21,151 --> 00:01:24,604
At the boot menu, you press e to edit the entry,

16
00:01:24,624 --> 00:01:29,741
add systemd.unit=TARGET at the end of the linux line, and press Ctrl+X.

17
00:01:29,761 --> 00:01:33,142
That boots once, and changes nothing permanent.

18
00:01:44,662 --> 00:01:51,174
First, look. systemctl get-default shows the target this machine normally boots into:

19
00:01:51,194 --> 00:01:52,557
multi-user.target.

20
00:01:57,027 --> 00:02:02,427
systemctl is-active asks if a target is running. multi-user is active.

21
00:02:02,447 --> 00:02:06,841
graphical is inactive, which is why the exit status is 3.

22
00:02:12,361 --> 00:02:14,846
Every boot has a kernel command line,

23
00:02:14,866 --> 00:02:18,095
the list of options the kernel was started with.

24
00:02:18,115 --> 00:02:20,938
You can read it in the file /proc/cmdline.

25
00:02:25,408 --> 00:02:29,625
It names the kernel image, the console, and the root file system.

26
00:02:29,645 --> 00:02:33,602
There is no systemd.unit here, so the default target is used.

27
00:02:39,122 --> 00:02:43,483
A virtual machine in the cloud has no keyboard for the boot menu,

28
00:02:43,503 --> 00:02:48,808
so we add the same option a different way. grubby edits the saved boot entries.

29
00:02:48,828 --> 00:02:51,774
The option is systemd.unit=graphical.target.

30
00:02:56,244 --> 00:03:01,519
grubby --info shows the default entry. The args line now ends with our option.

31
00:03:01,539 --> 00:03:05,931
Remember: this one is permanent, so we will remove it afterwards.

32
00:03:13,451 --> 00:03:16,887
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

33
00:03:18,407 --> 00:03:25,448
We reboot. After the boot, the kernel command line ends with systemd.unit=graphical.target.

34
00:03:31,968 --> 00:03:35,007
systemctl get-default still says multi-user,

35
00:03:35,027 --> 00:03:40,012
and it even tells us that the kernel command line overrides the default.

36
00:03:40,032 --> 00:03:42,445
And graphical.target is now active.

37
00:03:42,465 --> 00:03:46,894
This lab has no desktop installed, so the screen looks the same,

38
00:03:46,914 --> 00:03:48,353
but the target is up.

39
00:03:54,873 --> 00:03:55,849
Now we clean up.

40
00:03:55,869 --> 00:03:59,146
grubby with --remove-args takes the option out again,

41
00:03:59,166 --> 00:04:02,320
and the info output shows the args line without it.

42
00:04:06,790 --> 00:04:11,762
For rescue or emergency, the steps are the same, only the target name changes.

43
00:04:11,782 --> 00:04:14,770
On the exam you would press e at the boot menu.

44
00:04:14,790 --> 00:04:17,266
We cannot show that screen in this lab.

45
00:04:24,786 --> 00:04:31,756
Your turn. Add the option systemd.unit=graphical.target to the boot entries, and reboot.

46
00:04:31,776 --> 00:04:34,298
Then make a folder called lab21,

47
00:04:34,318 --> 00:04:40,811
and save the output of systemctl is-active graphical.target in a file, target.txt.

48
00:04:40,831 --> 00:04:43,433
Finally, remove the option again.

49
00:04:49,903 --> 00:04:52,720
Run the checker, and try before you open the solution.

50
00:04:58,240 --> 00:05:02,465
Next up: interrupt the boot process in order to gain access to a system.

51
00:05:02,935 --> 00:05:05,838
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
